##### MF1: GENERAL INFORMATION                                    1925 1451  225
                                                                  1925 1451  226
- MT451    : Descriptive data and directory                       1925 1451  227
                                                                  1925 1451  228
This text and the full directory of used MF/MT sections.          1925 1451  229
                                                                  1925 1451  230
##### MF2: RESONANCE PARAMETERS                                   1925 1451  231
                                                                  1925 1451  232
- MT151    : Resonance parameters                                 1925 1451  233
                                                                  1925 1451  234
Details about the origin of the resonance parameters for this     1925 1451  235
nuclide are given above in this description. In general, both a   1925 1451  236
Resolved and an Unresolved Resonance Range are given.             1925 1451  237
                                                                  1925 1451  238
##### MF3: REACTION CROSS SECTIONS                                1925 1451  239
                                                                  1925 1451  240
All the data present in the following MT-sections have been       1925 1451  241
calculated with TALYS, possibly normalized to experimental data.  1925 1451  242
If the maximal cross section in an excitation function over the   1925 1451  243
whole energy range does not exceed 1.e-9 b, the MT-number is not  1925 1451  244
included. Cross sections lower than 1.e-20 b are assumed to have  1925 1451  245
no physical meaning and are set to zero. All MT numbers extend up 1925 1451  246
to the highest incident energy.                                   1925 1451  247
The following reaction channels/MT numbers are generally included:1925 1451  248
                                                                  1925 1451  249
- MT1      : Total cross section                                  1925 1451  250
                                                                  1925 1451  251
Calculated with the optical model over the whole energy range,    1925 1451  252
or partly reproduced using experimental data.                     1925 1451  253
                                                                  1925 1451  254
- MT2      : Elastic scattering cross section                     1925 1451  255
                                                                  1925 1451  256
Obtained by subtracting the non-elastic cross section from the    1925 1451  257
total cross section.                                              1925 1451  258
                                                                  1925 1451  259
- MT3      : Non-elastic cross section                            1925 1451  260
                                                                  1925 1451  261
Calculated with the optical model over the whole energy range.    1925 1451  262
                                                                  1925 1451  263
- MT4      : Total inelastic cross section                        1925 1451  264
                                                                  1925 1451  265
Sum of MT=51-91.                                                  1925 1451  266
                                                                  1925 1451  267
- MT5      : (n,anything) cross section                           1925 1451  268
                                                                  1925 1451  269
MT5 contains the reactions which can not be stored in any other   1925 1451  270
MT-number. The information of MF3/MT5 can be combined with        1925 1451  271
MF6/MT5 to obtain residual production cross sections particle     1925 1451  272
production cross sections and (double-)differential cross         1925 1451  273
sections.                                                         1925 1451  274
                                                                  1925 1451  275
- MT16     : (n,2n) cross section                                 1925 1451  276
- MT17     : (n,3n) cross section                                 1925 1451  277
- MT22     : (n,na) cross section                                 1925 1451  278
- MT28     : (n,np) cross section                                 1925 1451  279
- MT51-90  : (n,n') cross section for 1st-40th excited state      1925 1451  280
- MT91     : (n,n') continuum cross section                       1925 1451  281
- MT102    : (n,gamma) cross section                              1925 1451  282
- MT103    : (n,p) cross section                                  1925 1451  283
- MT104    : (n,d) cross section                                  1925 1451  284
- MT105    : (n,t) cross section                                  1925 1451  285
- MT106    : (n,h) cross section                                  1925 1451  286
- MT107    : (n,a) cross section                                  1925 1451  287
- MT600-610: (n,p) cross section for 0th-10th excited state       1925 1451  288
- MT649    : (n,p) continuum cross section                        1925 1451  289
- MT650-655: (n,d) cross section for 0th-5th excited state        1925 1451  290
- MT699    : (n,d) continuum cross section                        1925 1451  291
- MT700-705: (n,t) cross section for 0th-5th excited state        1925 1451  292
- MT749    : (n,t) continuum cross section                        1925 1451  293
- MT750-755: (n,h) cross section for 0th-5th excited state        1925 1451  294
- MT799    : (n,h) continuum cross section                        1925 1451  295
- MT800-810: (n,a) cross section for 0th-10th excited state       1925 1451  296
- MT849    : (n,a) continuum cross section                        1925 1451  297
                                                                  1925 1451  298
##### MF4: ANGULAR DISTRIBUTIONS OF SECONDARY PARTICLES           1925 1451  299
                                                                  1925 1451  300
We use MF4 for the neutron scattering angular distribution for    1925 1451  301
discrete levels.                                                  1925 1451  302
                                                                  1925 1451  303
- MT2      : Elastic angular distribution                         1925 1451  304
                                                                  1925 1451  305
The flag LTT=3 is used to indicate a switch at some high energy,  1925 1451  306
generally 50 MeV, from a Legendre representation to a tabulated   1925 1451  307
representation. For incident energies below this energy, the      1925 1451  308
Legendre coefficients are given on a sufficiently precise energy  1925 1451  309
grid. They are a sum of calculated Legendre coefficients for      1925 1451  310
compound nucleus and shape-elastic scattering. For higher         1925 1451  311
incident energies, relative angular distributions are tabulated   1925 1451  312
on an angular grid.                                               1925 1451  313
                                                                  1925 1451  314
- MT51-70  : (n,n') angular distribution for 1st-20th state       1925 1451  315
- MT600-610: (n,p) angular distribution for 0th-10th state        1925 1451  316
- MT650-655: (n,d) angular distribution for 0th-5th state         1925 1451  317
- MT700-705: (n,t) angular distribution for 0th-5th state         1925 1451  318
- MT750-755: (n,h) angular distribution for 0th-5th state         1925 1451  319
- MT800-810: (n,a) angular distribution for 0th-10th state        1925 1451  320
                                                                  1925 1451  321
##### MF6: PRODUCT ENERGY-ANGLE DISTRIBUTIONS                     1925 1451  322
                                                                  1925 1451  323
In MF6 we store all secondary energy, angle, and energy-angle     1925 1451  324
distributions, as well as all residual and discrete + continuum   1925 1451  325
photon production cross sections for channels other than capture  1925 1451  326
(MT102) and reactions to discrete levels (MT51,etc.).             1925 1451  327
All data are generated with TALYS.                                1925 1451  328
                                                                  1925 1451  329
- MT5      : (n,anything) yields and energy-angle distributions   1925 1451  330
                                                                  1925 1451  331
MT5 contains, in this order,                                      1925 1451  332
For neutrons up to alpha particles and photons:                   1925 1451  333
- the production yields per particle for the whole energy range   1925 1451  334
- the secondary energy-angle distributions per particles which are1925 1451  335
  not represented by explicit MT numbers. On a secondary energy   1925 1451  336
  grid the relative emission spectra are given together with the  1925 1451  337
  parameters for the Kalbach systematics for angular              1925 1451  338
  distributions. Inelastic scattering cross sections for discrete 1925 1451  339
  states have been broadened and added to the continuum spectra.  1925 1451  340
For each residual product:                                        1925 1451  341
- the production yields for the whole energy range.               1925 1451  342
- the recoil                                                      1925 1451  343
These distributions can be multiplied with MF3/MT5 to get         1925 1451  344
the production cross sections and (double-)differential cross     1925 1451  345
sections.                                                         1925 1451  346
                                                                  1925 1451  347
- MT16     : (n,2n) energy-angle distr. and photon production     1925 1451  348
                                                                  1925 1451  349
First, for each type of outgoing particle, the (trivial) integer  1925 1451  350
particle yields are given. Next, on a sufficiently dense incident 1925 1451  351
energy grid the secondary energy-angle distributions are          1925 1451  352
specified by means of the relative emission spectra and the       1925 1451  353
parameters for the Kalbach systematics for angular distributions. 1925 1451  354
Next, the photon yield is tabulated as a function of incident     1925 1451  355
energy. For each incident energy, the photon production is given  1925 1451  356
for all discrete gamma lines present in the final nucleus. A      1925 1451  357
continuum photon distribution is added to this. We assume         1925 1451  358
isotropy for all produced gamma rays.                             1925 1451  359
                                                                  1925 1451  360
For the following MT-numbers, the same procedure as for MT16 is   1925 1451  361
followed:                                                         1925 1451  362
                                                                  1925 1451  363
- MT17 : (n,3n) energy-angle distr. and photon production         1925 1451  364
- MT22 : (n,na) energy-angle distr. and photon production         1925 1451  365
- MT28 : (n,np) energy-angle distr. and photon production         1925 1451  366
- MT91 : (n,n') continuum energy-angle distr. and phot. prod.     1925 1451  367
- MT649: (n,p) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     1925 1451  368
- MT699: (n,d) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     1925 1451  369
- MT749: (n,t) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     1925 1451  370
- MT809: (n,h) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     1925 1451  371
- MT849: (n,a) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     1925 1451  372
                                                                  1925 1451  373
##### MF8: RADIOACTIVE DECAY DATA                                 1925 1451  374
                                                                  1925 1451  375
For reactions to isomers, MF8 designates where the information    1925 1451  376
for isomeric versus ground state production, or residual          1925 1451  377
production can be found, i.e. for each MT number it points to     1925 1451  378
either MF6, MF9 or MF10.                                          1925 1451  379
                                                                  1925 1451  380
##### MF9: MULTIPLICITIES FOR PRODUCTION OF RADIOACTIVE NUCLIDES  1925 1451  381
                                                                  1925 1451  382
MF9 is used for isomer production by non-threshold reactions.     1925 1451  383
Final states with a lifetime that exceeds 1 second are treated    1925 1451  384
as isomer.                                                        1925 1451  385
                                                                  1925 1451  386
##### MF10: CROSS SECTIONS FOR PRODUCTION OF RADIOACTIVE NUCLIDES 1925 1451  387
                                                                  1925 1451  388
MF10 is used for isomer production by threshold reactions.        1925 1451  389
Final states with a lifetime that exceeds 1 second are treated    1925 1451  390
as isomer.                                                        1925 1451  391
                                                                  1925 1451  392
##### MF12: PHOTON PRODUCTION YIELD DATA                          1925 1451  393
                                                                  1925 1451  394
MF12 is used for the photon production for two types of reactions:1925 1451  395
- Reactions to discrete levels (MT51, etc.) For this transition   1925 1451  396
  probabilities are used (LO=2)                                   1925 1451  397
- Photons from neutron capture (MT102). Here multiplicities are   1925 1451  398
  given                                                           1925 1451  399
                                                                  1925 1451  400
##### MF14: PHOTON ANGULAR DISTRIBUTIONS                          1925 1451  401
                                                                  1925 1451  402
All photon production is considered as isotropic                  1925 1451  403
                                                                  1925 1451  404
##### MF15: PHOTON ENERGY SPECTRA                                 1925 1451  405
                                                                  1925 1451  406
For capture (MT102), the photon energy spectrum is given.         1925 1451  407
                                                                  1925 1451  408
##### MF32: COVARIANCES OF RESONANCE PARAMETERS                   1925 1451  409
                                                                  1925 1451  410
In MF32, we store the covariances of resonance parameters,        1925 1451  411
and more details are given above in this description. In most     1925 1451  412
versions of TENDL, this information is now stored in MF33.        1925 1451  413
                                                                  1925 1451  414
##### MF33: COVARIANCES OF NEUTRON CROSS SECTIONS                 1925 1451  415
                                                                  1925 1451  416
In MF33, we store the covariances of most, but not all, reaction  1925 1451  417
channels that are given in MF3. All covariances are generated     1925 1451  418
with the TASMAN code [kon19], which generates TALYS runs using    1925 1451  419
a Monte Carlo parameter method.                                   1925 1451  420
For each MT-number, the information consists of:                  1925 1451  421
1. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTa, Ea (variances)                1925 1451  422
2. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTa, Eb (correlations for the      1925 1451  423
   same reaction channel between different energies)              1925 1451  424
3. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTb, Ea (correlations between      1925 1451  425
   different reaction channels for the same energy)               1925 1451  426
4. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTb, Eb (correlations between      1925 1451  427
   different reaction channels for different energies)            1925 1451  428
Classes 1 and 2 are covered by NI-type subsubsections with        1925 1451  429
flag LB=5, followed by a LB=8 section which contains              1925 1451  430
the cross section variances multiplied by 1.e-3. Next, classes 3  1925 1451  431
and 4 are covered by flag LB=6 to account for the different       1925 1451  432
energy grids that two correlating MT-sections may have. Since     1925 1451  433
the total correlation matrix is fully symmetric, we only need to  1925 1451  434
include the correlation elements between different MT-numbers     1925 1451  435
once, and we do that in the subsection with the lowest of the     1925 1451  436
two MT-numbers. We include covariance information for all the     1925 1451  437
total cross sections, the lumped partial cross sections and the   1925 1451  438
individual inelastic cross sections per level (MT51,...). We do   1925 1451  439
not include covariances for MT600 and higher MT-numbers. For the  1925 1451  440
most important channels, we include cross-channel correlations.   1925 1451  441
                                                                  1925 1451  442
##### MF34: COVARIANCES OF ANGULAR DISTRIBUTIONS                  1925 1451  443
                                                                  1925 1451  444
In MF34, we store the covariances of the elastic scattering       1925 1451  445
angular distributions that are given in MF4/MT2. All covariances  1925 1451  446
are generated with the TASMAN code [kon19], which generates       1925 1451  447
TALYS runs using a Monte Carlo parameter method. The tables are   1925 1451  448
restricted to MT2 and contain covariances for the first 6         1925 1451  449
Legendre coefficients. Both intra- and inter-correlations of the  1925 1451  450
various Legendre coefficients are given. As in MF33, the LB5      1925 1451  451
representation is used.                                           1925 1451  452
                                                                  1925 1451  453
##### MF40: COVARIANCES OF ISOMER PRODUCTION                      1925 1451  454
                                                                  1925 1451  455
For covariances of ground state and isomeric cross sections, the  1925 1451  456
same procedures as for MF33 are used.                             1925 1451  457
                                                                  1925 1451  458
